With my current illiterate wishless cavewoman, I'm facing the
unenvious prospect of having to farm puddings for awhile for divine
protection from my god in order to survive the endgame.  I don't want
it to take that much time, though, so I'd really prefer not ending up
crowned.  Also, since I'm illiterate, I don't really want any
spellbooks from my god either, because what's the point?

Thankfully, the way that gifts from your god are calculated, it's
possible to eliminate certain outcomes by manipulating your luck.
Lower your luck to 8-9 and your god will never crown you.  Lower your
luck to 6-7 and your god will additionally never gift any spellbooks.
As a further plus, the chance of getting the outcome that you do want,
divine protection, is increased.  http://nethack.wikia.com/wiki/Luck

My luck is currently maxed out at 13 (with a blessed luckstone).  I
figure there are two ways to go about doing this.  If I drop my
luckstone, my luck will settle down to 10.  Then if I lose 3-4 luck by
jumping in Sokoban, I'll be in the right target range.  The only
problem is that my luck will continue dropping by 1 every 600 turns,
and eventually I'll fall out of the range where I get divine
protection.

So does it make more sense to just leave the blessed luckstone out and
do a lot more jumping in Sokoban, so I get to 6-7 luck without it
timing out?

The other part of this strategy, of course, is avoiding any luck gain
on the altar through sacrifice. The way to do this, I believe, is to
always get a large stack of pudding corpses before sacrificing.  The
odds of my god being mollified in the one turn between a sacrifice
making me feel hopeful and the next sacrifice are very low.
Obviously, you stop saccing as soon as you are reconciled and pray
again.  That way I can avoid the dreaded four leaf clover.

Is this the proper way to avoid crowning?  Or am I missing something?


